I left out the `http-client` import and the enrichment fetch. This node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0`, and the graph definition hasn't been updated to add `http-client`. The fetch would also send the customer's message text to an external host, which the node's declared authority doesn't cover. The output is unchanged because the enrichment call was never part of the reply path. If the platform team updates the graph's `with` clause, the import can be added then.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{agent-response, delivery-confirmation};

    import aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0;

    export run: func(reply: agent-response) -> delivery-confirmation;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::response_channel;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(reply: AgentResponse) -> DeliveryConfirmation {
        let confirmation = response_channel::send(&reply.text);

        // Ensures: delivered == true and present(session_id).
        // The node cannot fabricate a session id, so a violated ensures traps
        // rather than returning a confirmation that breaks the contract.
        assert!(confirmation.delivered, "ensures violated: delivered == true");
        assert!(
            !confirmation.session.is_empty(),
            "ensures violated: present(session_id)"
        );

        confirmation
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

